t 90 percent  less of what you made before  unfortunately yeah everything revolves  around this uh  currency your currency is worth less it  makes importing things much more  expensive for lebanon that's a massive  deal because it imports nearly  everything including all its fuel so now  on top of the currency crisis there's a  fuel crisis  the government simply can't afford to  buy enough and it's abandoned some of  the fuel subsidies that helped make fuel  affordable for ordinary people so now  you've got both shortages and super high  prices  so the fuel crisis is creating a domino  effect drinking water now costs four  times what it used to and the lights are  literally going out because there's no  fuel for generators constant  obstructions from  this is an example of electricity going  off you're talking about some places not  getting more than a couple of hours of  electricity a day this summer and when  it comes to hospitals when it comes to  people where you have young children or  elderly this is this is a health risk so  it only gets worse because now you've  got a health crisis too hospitals are  struggling the un has got involved even  supplying fuel to help keep generators  running and like other imports medicine  has become too expensive or hard to find  even ordinary drugs like paracetamol let  alone life-saving ones for diabetics or  cancer patients  so people like marina are stepping in  she set up a charity to coordinate  donations of medicine from abroad we  cross the red lines and  we're really facing a hard and harsh  times at the end this is not our job  this is the job of the state but since  we don't have a state we are doing this  job  that idea that the state is missing in  action well that's why things have  become so desperate lebanon is  definitely a rare case of a country with  virtually no viable state services  uh or social services or social  institutions the government has  completely imploded as an institution  delivery of basic services have  completely stopped and this isn't  exactly new it's almost as if lebanon is  in a permanent state of political crisis  and critics say part of the problem is  the system itself  now the way it works is that the three  biggest sects are guaranteed  representation that was the deal to keep  the peace after the civil war so for  example the president is always a  maronite christian the prime minister is  always sunni and the speaker of  parliament is shia but in reality it's  led to political gridlock infighting and  nothing really gets done it's really  about  you know  who gets which government sponsored  contracts what kind of company  affiliated with who um when you're  forming a government for example you  know it's not just about  who is the best person to handle the  ministry of economy it's whether the  president who is a marine christian  should pick that minister whether it's  the prime minister or somebody will pick  it  and it becomes a horse trading  situation you end up with a lot of  corruption and accusations that  politicians are only serving their own  interests not working for the good of  the country  a couple of years ago hundreds of  thousands of people protested to say as  much  [Music]  and after the horrific explosion in  beirut last year there was outrage  directed at politicians and so far no  one's been held to account  [ __ ]  what's happened recently is that the  political parties have finally agreed on  a new government after 13 months  lebanon's feuding politicians have now  agreed how to distribute power in a new  cabinets but most of the faces are  familiar like billionaire prime minister  najib makati who's had the job twice  already  he's trying to manage expectations but  they're already rock bottom i'm very  pessimistic about this government i  think this is this is just you know like  a morphine dose that was given to the  people in order to calm them down people  just feel that because this is a  returning prime minister this is his  third time as prime minister they say  well you know what doesn't matter if  they're calling the shots then we can't  expect the people that put us in this  crisis to get us out of it and there's a  lot that needs doing priority number one  is to get cash into the country and the  government's best bet is to secure a  bailout from the imf it's now planning  to reopen talks with them after the last  one stalled in 2020 but if the  government wants money it will also need  to present a recovery plan that includes  a bunch of reforms we need to  get dollars from the rest of the world  desperately in order to pump badly need  oxygen for the population but that only  stops the collapse this country has been  very badly managed for a very long time  we need a political transformation  if there's any chance of that happening  it could start with parliamentary  elections coming up in may but that's a  big if  just like fuel and medicine  hope that politicians will actually  change things is in seriously short  supply  if you found that useful check out our  recent explainer on al qaeda and click  that subscribe button so you don't miss  our next episode  [Music]

